module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FundamentalGroup.GaloisCategory.InertiaDiagram
public import
  StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FundamentalGroup.LocalConnectedness.FinitelyManyClosedPoints
public import StacksFrontier.Topology.Algebra.Group.ClosedNormalClosureComposition

/-!
# Removing finitely many closed points one at a time

Let `X` be a scheme, let `x₁, …, xₙ ∈ X` be finitely many closed points, and let
`U = X ∖ {x₁, …, xₙ}`. Stacks Tag 0BSC describes the kernel of `π₁(U) → π₁(X)` as the smallest
closed normal subgroup containing the images of the fundamental groups of the punctured spectra
of the strict henselizations of the `𝒪_{X,xᵢ}`. Its proof reduces the statement to the case of a
single removed point (Stacks 0BSB) along the chain of opens

```
U = X ∖ {x₁, …, xₙ} ⊆ ⋯ ⊆ X ∖ {x₁, x₂} ⊆ X ∖ {x₁} ⊆ X
```

and leaves the group theory of that reduction to the reader. This file carries it out.

For a family of closed points `x : Fin n → X` the opens
`AlgebraicGeometry.chainOpen x hclosed j = X ∖ {xᵢ : j ≤ i}` increase from `U` at `j = 0` to `X`
at `j = n`, each step adding back one point. If `X` is connected and `U` is connected and
retrocompact in `X`, then every stage is connected and retrocompact in `X`, and every step is
surjective on étale fundamental groups.

For an increasing chain of connected schemes `B 0 ⟶ B 1 ⟶ ⋯` and a geometric point `u` of `B 0`,
suppose every step is surjective on étale fundamental groups and has as kernel the smallest
closed normal subgroup containing a set `S j`. Then the kernel of `π₁(B 0, u) ⟶ π₁(B n, ·)` is the
smallest closed normal subgroup containing the union of the preimages of the `S j`. If moreover
each `S j` is the image of a map `m j : L j → π₁(B 0, u)`, then the kernel is generated by the
union of the ranges of the `m j`. This is the form of Stacks 0BSC: there `L j` is the
fundamental group of the punctured spectrum of the strict henselization at `xⱼ`, which maps into
`X` with image among the generalizations of `xⱼ`, hence into `U`.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.chainOpen`: the chain of opens `X ∖ {xᵢ : j ≤ i}` attached to a finite
  family of closed points.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.chainPoint`: the base points of an increasing chain of schemes, the
  successive images of a geometric point of the bottom stage.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.chainGroupMap`: the composite `π₁(B 0, u) ⟶ π₁(B j, ·)` of the first `j`
  maps of étale fundamental groups along the chain.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.coe_chainOpen_zero`, `AlgebraicGeometry.chainOpen_eq_top`: the bottom of
  the chain is `U` and the top is `X`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.connectedSpace_chainOpen`: every stage of the chain is connected.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.quasiCompact_chainOpen_ι`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.quasiCompact_chainOpen_homOfLE`: every stage is retrocompact in `X`, and
  every step of the chain is quasi-compact.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.surjective_etaleFundamentalGroupMap_chainOpen_homOfLE`: every step of the
  chain is surjective on étale fundamental groups.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.chainGroupMap_heq_succ`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.ker_chainGroupMap_eq_ker_etaleFundamentalGroupMap_succ`: the composite of
  the maps along the chain is the map induced by the inclusion of the bottom stage, and the two
  have the same kernel.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ker_chainGroupMap_eq_closedNormalClosure_biUnion_preimage`: the kernel of
  the composite is generated by the preimages of the generating sets of the steps.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ker_chainGroupMap_eq_closedNormalClosure_biUnion_range`: the kernel of the
  composite is generated by the union of the ranges of maps into `π₁(B 0, u)` whose images
  generate the kernels of the steps.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.isConnected_coe_opens`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.connectedSpace_of_isConnected_coe_opens`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.quasiCompact_ι_iff_isRetrocompact`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.quasiCompact_homOfLE`, `AlgebraicGeometry.range_homOfLE`: general facts
  about open subschemes.

## Implementation notes

The two reduction theorems take the one-point kernel description `hker` for each step as a
hypothesis, so they do not discharge the kernel clause of Stacks 0BSC.

`[ConnectedSpace X]` is assumed: the density of `U` in `X`, from which the connectedness of `X`
would follow, is itself proved from it.

The connectedness hypothesis `hconnected` of
`AlgebraicGeometry.surjective_etaleFundamentalGroupMap_chainOpen_homOfLE` is stated at the stalks
of the stage `chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)` rather than at the stalks of `X`; the two agree
because an open immersion is an isomorphism on stalks.

The chain is indexed by `ℕ`; a family of `n` points gives `chainOpen x hclosed j = ⊤` for every
`j ≥ n`. The abstract chain runs upwards, `B 0 ⟶ B 1 ⟶ ⋯`, and the base point of the `j`-th
stage is defined as the image of `u`, so the codomain of
`etaleFundamentalGroupMap (d j) (chainPoint d u j)` is definitionally
`π₁(B (j + 1), chainPoint d u (j + 1))` and no transport of base points is needed.

`AlgebraicGeometry.chainGroupMap_heq_succ` is stated for `j + 1` only: at `j = 0` it would need
`etaleFundamentalGroupMap (𝟙 X) x = id`, which is not proved here. Its heterogeneous equality
compares two geometric points of `B (j + 1)` which are only propositionally equal, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.chainPoint_eq_map_chainIncl`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BSC](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BSC)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BSB](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BSB)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BS9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BS9)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BSA](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BSA)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory Stacks.FundamentalGroup

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-! ### Open subschemes: connectedness, retrocompactness, and inclusions -/

section Opens

variable {X : Scheme.{u}}

/-- An open subscheme whose underlying space is connected is a connected subset of the ambient
scheme. -/
theorem isConnected_coe_opens (V : X.Opens) [ConnectedSpace V.toScheme] :
    _root_.IsConnected (V : Set X) := by
  have h := _root_.isConnected_range (f := V.ι.base) V.ι.continuous
  rwa [Scheme.Opens.range_ι] at h

/-- An open of a scheme which is connected as a subset of the ambient space is connected as a
scheme. -/
theorem connectedSpace_of_isConnected_coe_opens (V : X.Opens)
    (h : _root_.IsConnected (V : Set X)) : ConnectedSpace V.toScheme := by
  obtain ⟨⟨y, hy⟩, hpre⟩ := h
  refine connectedSpace_iff_univ.mpr ⟨⟨⟨y, hy⟩, trivial⟩, ?_⟩
  rwa [← V.ι.isOpenEmbedding.isInducing.isPreconnected_image, Set.image_univ,
    Scheme.Opens.range_ι]

/-- The inclusion of a smaller open into the ambient scheme factors through a larger open. -/
private theorem ι_base_eq_homOfLE_base {U V : X.Opens} (e : U ≤ V) (p : U.toScheme) :
    U.ι.base p = V.ι.base ((X.homOfLE e).base p) := by
  conv_lhs => rw [← X.homOfLE_ι e]
  rfl

/-- The image of the inclusion of one open of a scheme into a larger one is the preimage of the
smaller open in the larger. -/
theorem range_homOfLE {U V : X.Opens} (e : U ≤ V) :
    Set.range (X.homOfLE e).base = V.ι.base ⁻¹' (U : Set X) := by
  apply Set.eq_of_subset_of_subset
  · rintro _ ⟨p, rfl⟩
    show V.ι.base ((X.homOfLE e).base p) ∈ (U : Set X)
    rw [← ι_base_eq_homOfLE_base e p, ← Scheme.Opens.range_ι U]
    exact Set.mem_range_self p
  · intro q hq
    have hmem : V.ι.base q ∈ (U : Set X) := hq
    exact ⟨(⟨V.ι.base q, hmem⟩ : U.toScheme),
      V.ι.isOpenEmbedding.injective (ι_base_eq_homOfLE_base e _).symm⟩

/-- **An open of a scheme is retrocompact exactly when its inclusion is quasi-compact.** -/
theorem quasiCompact_ι_iff_isRetrocompact (V : X.Opens) :
    QuasiCompact V.ι ↔ IsRetrocompact (V : Set X) := by
  refine ⟨fun hq W hWcompact hWopen ↦ ?_, fun h ↦ ⟨fun W hWopen hWcompact ↦ ?_⟩⟩
  · have h := QuasiCompact.isCompact_preimage (f := V.ι) W hWopen hWcompact
    rwa [V.ι.isOpenEmbedding.isEmbedding.isCompact_iff, Set.image_preimage_eq_range_inter,
      Scheme.Opens.range_ι] at h
  · rw [V.ι.isOpenEmbedding.isEmbedding.isCompact_iff, Set.image_preimage_eq_range_inter,
      Scheme.Opens.range_ι]
    exact h hWcompact hWopen

/-- The inclusion of one open of a scheme into a larger one is quasi-compact as soon as the
inclusion of the smaller one into the ambient scheme is. -/
theorem quasiCompact_homOfLE {U V : X.Opens} (e : U ≤ V) [QuasiCompact U.ι] :
    QuasiCompact (X.homOfLE e) := by
  refine ⟨fun W hWopen hWcompact => ?_⟩
  have himg : IsOpen (V.ι.base '' W) := V.ι.isOpenEmbedding.isOpenMap W hWopen
  have hcimg : IsCompact (V.ι.base '' W) := hWcompact.image V.ι.continuous
  have h := QuasiCompact.isCompact_preimage (f := U.ι) (V.ι.base '' W) himg hcimg
  have hset : U.ι.base ⁻¹' (V.ι.base '' W) = (X.homOfLE e).base ⁻¹' W := by
    ext p
    simp only [Set.mem_preimage, ι_base_eq_homOfLE_base e p]
    exact ⟨fun ⟨q, hq, hqe⟩ => by rwa [V.ι.isOpenEmbedding.injective hqe] at hq,
      fun hp => ⟨(X.homOfLE e).base p, hp, rfl⟩⟩
  rwa [hset] at h

/-- A finite subset of a topological space is retrocompact. -/
private theorem isRetrocompact_of_finite {F : Set X} (hF : F.Finite) : IsRetrocompact F := by
  rw [← Set.biUnion_of_singleton F]
  exact IsRetrocompact.biUnion hF fun _ _ => IsRetrocompact.singleton

end Opens

/-! ### The chain of fundamental groups of an increasing chain of schemes -/

section AbstractChain

variable {B : ℕ → Scheme.{u}}

/-- The base points of an increasing chain of schemes `d j : B j ⟶ B (j + 1)` determined by a
geometric point `u` of the bottom stage: the `j`-th one is the image of `u` in `B j`, defined by
the recursion `chainPoint d u (j + 1) = (chainPoint d u j).map (d j)`. -/
def chainPoint (d : ∀ j, B j ⟶ B (j + 1)) (u : GeometricPoint (B 0)) :
    (j : ℕ) → GeometricPoint (B j)
  | 0 => u
  | j + 1 => (chainPoint d u j).map (d j)

/-- The inclusion of the bottom stage of a chain of schemes into the `j`-th stage, that is, the
composite `d (j - 1) ∘ ⋯ ∘ d 0` of its first `j` steps. For `j = 0` it is the identity. -/
def chainIncl (d : ∀ j, B j ⟶ B (j + 1)) : (j : ℕ) → (B 0 ⟶ B j)
  | 0 => CategoryStruct.id (B 0)
  | j + 1 => chainIncl d j ≫ d j

/-- The `j`-th base point of a chain is the image of the bottom base point under the inclusion of
the bottom stage into the `j`-th stage. -/
theorem chainPoint_eq_map_chainIncl (d : ∀ j, B j ⟶ B (j + 1)) (u : GeometricPoint (B 0))
    (j : ℕ) : chainPoint d u j = u.map (chainIncl d j) := by
  induction j with
  | zero => cases u; rfl
  | succ j ih =>
    show (chainPoint d u j).map (d j) = u.map (chainIncl d j ≫ d j)
    rw [ih, GeometricPoint.map_map]

variable [∀ j, ConnectedSpace (B j)]

/-- The composite `π₁(B 0, u) ⟶ π₁(B j, ·)` of the first `j` maps of étale fundamental groups
along a chain of connected schemes, with the base points of
`AlgebraicGeometry.chainPoint`. For `j = 0` it is the identity. -/
def chainGroupMap (d : ∀ j, B j ⟶ B (j + 1)) (u : GeometricPoint (B 0)) :
    (j : ℕ) → (EtaleFundamentalGroup (B 0) u →ₜ* EtaleFundamentalGroup (B j) (chainPoint d u j))
  | 0 => ContinuousMonoidHom.id _
  | j + 1 => (etaleFundamentalGroupMap (d j) (chainPoint d u j)).comp (chainGroupMap d u j)

/-- The map of étale fundamental groups induced by a morphism respects a heterogeneous equality
between elements of the fundamental groups at two equal geometric points of its source. -/
private theorem heq_etaleFundamentalGroupMap_of_heq {Y Z : Scheme.{u}} [ConnectedSpace Y]
    [ConnectedSpace Z] (g : Y ⟶ Z) {p q : GeometricPoint Y} (hpq : p = q)
    {a : EtaleFundamentalGroup Y p} {b : EtaleFundamentalGroup Y q} (h : HEq a b) :
    HEq (etaleFundamentalGroupMap g p a) (etaleFundamentalGroupMap g q b) := by
  subst hpq
  rw [eq_of_heq h]

/-- Two heterogeneously equal elements of the étale fundamental groups at two equal geometric
points are trivial together. -/
private theorem eq_one_iff_heq_etaleFundamentalGroup {Y : Scheme.{u}} [ConnectedSpace Y]
    {p q : GeometricPoint Y} (hpq : p = q) {a : EtaleFundamentalGroup Y p}
    {b : EtaleFundamentalGroup Y q} (h : HEq a b) : a = 1 ↔ b = 1 := by
  subst hpq
  rw [eq_of_heq h]

/-- **Functoriality along the chain.** The composite of the first `j + 1` maps of fundamental
groups of a chain is the map induced by the inclusion of the bottom stage into the `(j + 1)`-st
stage. The equality is heterogeneous because the two geometric points of `B (j + 1)` involved
are only propositionally equal, by `AlgebraicGeometry.chainPoint_eq_map_chainIncl`. -/
theorem chainGroupMap_heq_succ (d : ∀ j, B j ⟶ B (j + 1)) (u : GeometricPoint (B 0)) (j : ℕ)
    (σ : EtaleFundamentalGroup (B 0) u) :
    HEq (chainGroupMap d u (j + 1) σ) (etaleFundamentalGroupMap (chainIncl d (j + 1)) u σ) := by
  induction j with
  | zero => exact HEq.refl _
  | succ j ih =>
    exact (heq_etaleFundamentalGroupMap_of_heq (d (j + 1))
      (chainPoint_eq_map_chainIncl d u (j + 1)) ih).trans
        (etaleFundamentalGroupMap_comp (chainIncl d (j + 1)) (d (j + 1)) u σ)

/-- The kernel of the composite of the first `j + 1` maps of fundamental groups of a chain is the
kernel of the map induced by the inclusion of the bottom stage into the `(j + 1)`-st stage. -/
theorem ker_chainGroupMap_eq_ker_etaleFundamentalGroupMap_succ (d : ∀ j, B j ⟶ B (j + 1))
    (u : GeometricPoint (B 0)) (j : ℕ) :
    MonoidHom.ker (chainGroupMap d u (j + 1)).toMonoidHom =
      MonoidHom.ker (etaleFundamentalGroupMap (chainIncl d (j + 1)) u).toMonoidHom := by
  ext σ
  simp only [MonoidHom.mem_ker]
  exact eq_one_iff_heq_etaleFundamentalGroup (chainPoint_eq_map_chainIncl d u (j + 1))
    (chainGroupMap_heq_succ d u j σ)

/-- If every step of a chain is surjective on étale fundamental groups then so is the composite
of its first `j` steps. -/
theorem surjective_chainGroupMap (d : ∀ j, B j ⟶ B (j + 1)) (u : GeometricPoint (B 0))
    (hsurjective : ∀ j, Function.Surjective (etaleFundamentalGroupMap (d j) (chainPoint d u j)))
    (j : ℕ) : Function.Surjective (chainGroupMap d u j) := by
  induction j with
  | zero => exact Function.surjective_id
  | succ j ih => exact (hsurjective j).comp ih

/-- The composite of the first `j` maps of fundamental groups of a chain is a closed map. -/
private theorem isClosedMap_chainGroupMap (d : ∀ j, B j ⟶ B (j + 1)) (u : GeometricPoint (B 0))
    (j : ℕ) : IsClosedMap (chainGroupMap d u j) :=
  have := compactSpace_etaleFundamentalGroup (B 0) u
  have := t2Space_etaleFundamentalGroup (B j) (chainPoint d u j)
  (map_continuous (chainGroupMap d u j)).isClosedMap

/-- The smallest closed normal subgroup of an étale fundamental group containing the empty set is
trivial. -/
private theorem closedNormalClosure_empty_etaleFundamentalGroup (u : GeometricPoint (B 0)) :
    (Subgroup.normalClosure
      (∅ : Set (EtaleFundamentalGroup (B 0) u))).topologicalClosure = ⊥ := by
  have := t2Space_etaleFundamentalGroup (B 0) u
  rw [Subgroup.normalClosure_empty]
  refine SetLike.coe_injective ?_
  rw [Subgroup.coe_topologicalClosure_bot, Subgroup.coe_bot, closure_singleton]

/-- The union over `j < n + 1` is the union over `j < n` together with the `n`-th term. -/
private theorem biUnion_range_succ_eq_union {α : Type*} (f : ℕ → Set α) (n : ℕ) :
    (⋃ j ∈ Finset.range (n + 1), f j) = (⋃ j ∈ Finset.range n, f j) ∪ f n := by
  rw [Finset.range_add_one, Finset.set_biUnion_insert, Set.union_comm]

/-- The kernel of the composite of no maps of a chain is the closed normal closure of the empty
union. -/
private theorem ker_chainGroupMap_zero_eq (d : ∀ j, B j ⟶ B (j + 1)) (u : GeometricPoint (B 0))
    (f : ℕ → Set (EtaleFundamentalGroup (B 0) u)) :
    MonoidHom.ker (chainGroupMap d u 0).toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (⋃ j ∈ Finset.range 0, f j)).topologicalClosure := by
  simp only [Finset.range_zero, Finset.notMem_empty, Set.iUnion_of_empty, Set.iUnion_empty,
    closedNormalClosure_empty_etaleFundamentalGroup]
  exact MonoidHom.ker_id

/-- **The reduction along the chain, with preimages.**

Let `d j : B j ⟶ B (j + 1)` be an increasing chain of connected schemes, `u` a geometric point
of `B 0`. Suppose that for every `j` the induced map `π₁(B j, ·) ⟶ π₁(B (j + 1), ·)` is
surjective and that its kernel is the smallest closed normal subgroup containing a set
`S j ⊆ π₁(B j, ·)`. Then the kernel of the composite `π₁(B 0, u) ⟶ π₁(B n, ·)` of the first `n`
steps is the smallest closed normal subgroup of `π₁(B 0, u)` containing the union, over `j < n`,
of the preimages of the `S j`. -/
theorem ker_chainGroupMap_eq_closedNormalClosure_biUnion_preimage
    (d : ∀ j, B j ⟶ B (j + 1)) (u : GeometricPoint (B 0))
    (S : ∀ j, Set (EtaleFundamentalGroup (B j) (chainPoint d u j)))
    (hsurjective : ∀ j, Function.Surjective (etaleFundamentalGroupMap (d j) (chainPoint d u j)))
    (hker : ∀ j, MonoidHom.ker (etaleFundamentalGroupMap (d j) (chainPoint d u j)).toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (S j)).topologicalClosure)
    (n : ℕ) :
    MonoidHom.ker (chainGroupMap d u n).toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure
        (⋃ j ∈ Finset.range n, chainGroupMap d u j ⁻¹' S j)).topologicalClosure := by
  induction n with
  | zero => exact ker_chainGroupMap_zero_eq d u _
  | succ n ih =>
    rw [biUnion_range_succ_eq_union fun j => chainGroupMap d u j ⁻¹' S j]
    exact ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure (chainGroupMap d u n)
      (etaleFundamentalGroupMap (d n) (chainPoint d u n))
      (surjective_chainGroupMap d u hsurjective n) (isClosedMap_chainGroupMap d u n) ih (hker n)

/-- **The reduction along the chain, with images.**

Let `d j : B j ⟶ B (j + 1)` be an increasing chain of connected schemes and `u` a geometric point
of `B 0`. Suppose given, for every `j`, a topological group `L j` and a continuous homomorphism
`m j : L j ⟶ π₁(B 0, u)` such that the induced map `π₁(B j, ·) ⟶ π₁(B (j + 1), ·)` is surjective
and its kernel is the smallest closed normal subgroup containing the image of `L j` in
`π₁(B j, ·)` *along* `m j`. Then the kernel of the composite `π₁(B 0, u) ⟶ π₁(B n, ·)` of the
first `n` steps is the smallest closed normal subgroup of `π₁(B 0, u)` containing the union, over
`j < n`, of the images of the `m j`. -/
theorem ker_chainGroupMap_eq_closedNormalClosure_biUnion_range
    (d : ∀ j, B j ⟶ B (j + 1)) (u : GeometricPoint (B 0))
    {L : ℕ → Type*} [∀ j, Group (L j)] [∀ j, TopologicalSpace (L j)]
    (m : ∀ j, L j →ₜ* EtaleFundamentalGroup (B 0) u)
    (hsurjective : ∀ j, Function.Surjective (etaleFundamentalGroupMap (d j) (chainPoint d u j)))
    (hker : ∀ j, MonoidHom.ker (etaleFundamentalGroupMap (d j) (chainPoint d u j)).toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure
        (Set.range ((chainGroupMap d u j).comp (m j)))).topologicalClosure)
    (n : ℕ) :
    MonoidHom.ker (chainGroupMap d u n).toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (⋃ j ∈ Finset.range n, Set.range (m j))).topologicalClosure := by
  induction n with
  | zero => exact ker_chainGroupMap_zero_eq d u _
  | succ n ih =>
    have himg : chainGroupMap d u n '' Set.range (m n) =
        Set.range ((chainGroupMap d u n).comp (m n)) := by
      show chainGroupMap d u n '' Set.range (m n) = Set.range (chainGroupMap d u n ∘ m n)
      exact (Set.range_comp _ _).symm
    rw [biUnion_range_succ_eq_union fun j => Set.range (m j)]
    exact ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure_of_image_eq (chainGroupMap d u n)
      (etaleFundamentalGroupMap (d n) (chainPoint d u n))
      (surjective_chainGroupMap d u hsurjective n) (isClosedMap_chainGroupMap d u n) himg ih
      (hker n)

end AbstractChain

/-! ### The chain of opens obtained by removing finitely many closed points one at a time -/

section ConcreteChain

variable {X : Scheme.{u}} {n : ℕ}

/-- The points of `x : Fin n → X` still removed at stage `j`, namely the `x i` with `j ≤ i`. -/
def chainRemoved (x : Fin n → X) (j : ℕ) : Set X := x '' {i : Fin n | j ≤ (i : ℕ)}

/-- Only finitely many points are removed at any stage. -/
theorem finite_chainRemoved (x : Fin n → X) (j : ℕ) : (chainRemoved x j).Finite :=
  Set.Finite.image _ (Set.toFinite _)

/-- The points removed at stage `j` are among the `x i`. -/
theorem chainRemoved_subset_range (x : Fin n → X) (j : ℕ) :
    chainRemoved x j ⊆ Set.range x := Set.image_subset_range _ _

/-- At stage `0` all of the points are removed. -/
theorem chainRemoved_zero (x : Fin n → X) : chainRemoved x 0 = Set.range x := by
  simp [chainRemoved]

/-- The removed sets shrink along the chain: passing from stage `j` to stage `j + 1` adds back
the point `x j`, if there is one. -/
theorem chainRemoved_succ_subset (x : Fin n → X) (j : ℕ) :
    chainRemoved x (j + 1) ⊆ chainRemoved x j :=
  Set.image_mono fun _ hi => Nat.le_of_succ_le hi

/-- From stage `n` on, no point is removed any more. -/
theorem chainRemoved_eq_empty (x : Fin n → X) {j : ℕ} (h : n ≤ j) : chainRemoved x j = ∅ := by
  refine Set.eq_empty_iff_forall_notMem.2 ?_
  rintro _ ⟨i, hi, rfl⟩
  exact absurd (lt_of_lt_of_le i.2 h) (not_lt.2 hi)

/-- A finite union of closed points is closed, so the set removed at any stage is closed. -/
theorem isClosed_chainRemoved (x : Fin n → X) (hclosed : ∀ i, IsClosed ({x i} : Set X))
    (j : ℕ) : IsClosed (chainRemoved x j) := by
  rw [← Set.biUnion_of_singleton (chainRemoved x j)]
  refine (finite_chainRemoved x j).isClosed_biUnion ?_
  rintro _ ⟨i, _, rfl⟩
  exact hclosed i

/-- **The chain of opens of Stacks 0BSC.** The `j`-th stage of the chain attached to a finite
family `x : Fin n → X` of closed points is the open `X ∖ {xᵢ : j ≤ i}`. It is `X ∖ {x₀, …, xₙ₋₁}`
at `j = 0` and all of `X` from `j = n` on, and each step adds back one point. -/
def chainOpen (x : Fin n → X) (hclosed : ∀ i, IsClosed ({x i} : Set X)) (j : ℕ) : X.Opens :=
  ⟨(chainRemoved x j)ᶜ, (isClosed_chainRemoved x hclosed j).isOpen_compl⟩

variable (x : Fin n → X) (hclosed : ∀ i, IsClosed ({x i} : Set X))

/-- The underlying set of the `j`-th stage of the chain is the complement of the set removed at
stage `j`. -/
theorem coe_chainOpen (j : ℕ) :
    ((chainOpen x hclosed j : X.Opens) : Set X) = (chainRemoved x j)ᶜ := rfl

/-- The chain of opens is increasing. -/
theorem chainOpen_le_succ (j : ℕ) : chainOpen x hclosed j ≤ chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1) :=
  Set.compl_subset_compl.2 (chainRemoved_succ_subset x j)

/-- Every stage of the chain contains the bottom stage. -/
theorem chainOpen_zero_le (j : ℕ) : chainOpen x hclosed 0 ≤ chainOpen x hclosed j :=
  Set.compl_subset_compl.2 (by rw [chainRemoved_zero]; exact chainRemoved_subset_range x j)

/-- The bottom of the chain is the complement `U = X ∖ {x₀, …, xₙ₋₁}` of all the points. -/
theorem coe_chainOpen_zero : ((chainOpen x hclosed 0 : X.Opens) : Set X) = (Set.range x)ᶜ := by
  rw [coe_chainOpen, chainRemoved_zero]

/-- From stage `n` on the chain is all of `X`. -/
theorem chainOpen_eq_top {j : ℕ} (h : n ≤ j) : chainOpen x hclosed j = ⊤ := by
  refine TopologicalSpace.Opens.ext ?_
  rw [coe_chainOpen, chainRemoved_eq_empty x h]
  simp

/-- The complement of the image of the `j`-th step of the chain, inside the `(j + 1)`-st stage,
is the preimage there of the set removed at stage `j`. -/
theorem compl_range_chainOpen_homOfLE (j : ℕ) :
    (Set.range (X.homOfLE (chainOpen_le_succ x hclosed j)).base)ᶜ =
      (chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).ι.base ⁻¹' chainRemoved x j := by
  rw [range_homOfLE, ← Set.preimage_compl, coe_chainOpen, compl_compl]

/-- Each step of the chain adds back only finitely many points. -/
theorem finite_compl_range_chainOpen_homOfLE (j : ℕ) :
    ((Set.range (X.homOfLE (chainOpen_le_succ x hclosed j)).base)ᶜ).Finite := by
  rw [compl_range_chainOpen_homOfLE]
  exact (finite_chainRemoved x j).preimage
    (chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).ι.isOpenEmbedding.injective.injOn

/-- Each of the points added back at a step of the chain is a closed point of the stage it is
added to. -/
theorem isClosed_singleton_of_notMem_range_chainOpen_homOfLE (j : ℕ)
    (y : (chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).toScheme)
    (hy : y ∉ Set.range (X.homOfLE (chainOpen_le_succ x hclosed j)).base) :
    IsClosed ({y} : Set (chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).toScheme) := by
  rw [← Set.mem_compl_iff, compl_range_chainOpen_homOfLE, Set.mem_preimage] at hy
  obtain ⟨i, -, hi⟩ := hy
  have hset : ({y} : Set (chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).toScheme) =
      (chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).ι.base ⁻¹' ({x i} : Set X) := by
    ext z
    simp only [Set.mem_singleton_iff, Set.mem_preimage]
    exact ⟨fun hz => by rw [hz]; exact hi.symm, fun hz =>
      (chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).ι.isOpenEmbedding.injective (hz.trans hi)⟩
  rw [hset]
  exact (hclosed i).preimage (chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).ι.continuous

end ConcreteChain

/-! ### Retrocompactness of every stage of the chain -/

section Retrocompact

variable {X : Scheme.{u}} {n : ℕ} (x : Fin n → X) (hclosed : ∀ i, IsClosed ({x i} : Set X))
  [QuasiCompact (chainOpen x hclosed 0).ι]

/-- **Every stage of the chain is retrocompact in `X`**, given that the bottom stage `U` is. -/
theorem isRetrocompact_coe_chainOpen (j : ℕ) :
    IsRetrocompact ((chainOpen x hclosed j : X.Opens) : Set X) := by
  have hsplit : ((chainOpen x hclosed j : X.Opens) : Set X) =
      ((chainOpen x hclosed 0 : X.Opens) : Set X) ∪ ((chainRemoved x j)ᶜ ∩ Set.range x) := by
    rw [coe_chainOpen, coe_chainOpen_zero, ← Set.diff_compl,
      Set.union_diff_cancel (Set.compl_subset_compl.mpr (chainRemoved_subset_range x j))]
  rw [hsplit]
  exact ((quasiCompact_ι_iff_isRetrocompact _).mp inferInstance).union
    (isRetrocompact_of_finite ((Set.finite_range x).inter_of_right _))

/-- **Every stage of the chain is retrocompact**, in the form of quasi-compactness of its
inclusion into `X`. -/
theorem quasiCompact_chainOpen_ι (j : ℕ) : QuasiCompact (chainOpen x hclosed j).ι :=
  (quasiCompact_ι_iff_isRetrocompact _).mpr (isRetrocompact_coe_chainOpen x hclosed j)

/-- Every step of the chain is quasi-compact. -/
theorem quasiCompact_chainOpen_homOfLE (j : ℕ) :
    QuasiCompact (X.homOfLE (chainOpen_le_succ x hclosed j)) :=
  have := quasiCompact_chainOpen_ι x hclosed j
  quasiCompact_homOfLE _

end Retrocompact

/-! ### Connectedness of every stage of the chain -/

section Connected

variable {X : Scheme.{u}} {n : ℕ} (x : Fin n → X) (hclosed : ∀ i, IsClosed ({x i} : Set X))
  [ConnectedSpace (chainOpen x hclosed 0).toScheme] [ConnectedSpace X]

/-- **The bottom stage of the chain is dense in `X`.** -/
theorem dense_coe_chainOpen_zero : Dense ((chainOpen x hclosed 0 : X.Opens) : Set X) := by
  have hdom : IsDominant (chainOpen x hclosed 0).ι := by
    refine isDominant_of_finite_isClosed_compl _ ?_ ?_
    · rw [Scheme.Opens.range_ι, coe_chainOpen_zero, compl_compl]
      exact Set.finite_range x
    · intro y hy
      rw [Scheme.Opens.range_ι, coe_chainOpen_zero] at hy
      obtain ⟨i, rfl⟩ := not_not.mp hy
      exact hclosed i
  have h : Dense (Set.range (chainOpen x hclosed 0).ι.base) :=
    (chainOpen x hclosed 0).ι.denseRange
  rwa [Scheme.Opens.range_ι] at h

/-- **Every stage of the chain is a connected subset of `X`.** -/
theorem isConnected_coe_chainOpen (j : ℕ) :
    _root_.IsConnected ((chainOpen x hclosed j : X.Opens) : Set X) := by
  have h0 := isConnected_coe_opens (chainOpen x hclosed 0)
  exact ⟨h0.nonempty.mono (chainOpen_zero_le x hclosed j),
    h0.isPreconnected.subset_closure (chainOpen_zero_le x hclosed j)
      ((dense_coe_chainOpen_zero x hclosed).closure_eq ▸ Set.subset_univ _)⟩

/-- **Every stage of the chain is connected.** -/
theorem connectedSpace_chainOpen (j : ℕ) : ConnectedSpace (chainOpen x hclosed j).toScheme :=
  connectedSpace_of_isConnected_coe_opens _ (isConnected_coe_chainOpen x hclosed j)

end Connected

/-! ### Surjectivity along every step of the chain -/

section Surjective

variable {X : Scheme.{u}} {n : ℕ} (x : Fin n → X) (hclosed : ∀ i, IsClosed ({x i} : Set X))
  [ConnectedSpace (chainOpen x hclosed 0).toScheme] [ConnectedSpace X]
  [QuasiCompact (chainOpen x hclosed 0).ι]

/-- **Every step of the chain is surjective on étale fundamental groups**, provided the
punctured spectra of the strict henselizations of the local rings, at the points the step adds
back, are connected. -/
theorem surjective_etaleFundamentalGroupMap_chainOpen_homOfLE (j : ℕ)
    (hconnected : ∀ y : (chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).toScheme,
      y ∉ Set.range (X.homOfLE (chainOpen_le_succ x hclosed j)).base →
        IsConnectedPuncturedStrictHenselization
          ((chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).toScheme.presheaf.stalk y))
    (w : GeometricPoint (chainOpen x hclosed j).toScheme) :
    letI : ConnectedSpace (chainOpen x hclosed j).toScheme := connectedSpace_chainOpen x hclosed j
    letI : ConnectedSpace (chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).toScheme :=
      connectedSpace_chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)
    Function.Surjective
      (etaleFundamentalGroupMap (X.homOfLE (chainOpen_le_succ x hclosed j)) w) := by
  have : ConnectedSpace (chainOpen x hclosed j).toScheme := connectedSpace_chainOpen x hclosed j
  have : ConnectedSpace (chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)).toScheme :=
    connectedSpace_chainOpen x hclosed (j + 1)
  have : QuasiCompact (X.homOfLE (chainOpen_le_succ x hclosed j)) :=
    quasiCompact_chainOpen_homOfLE x hclosed j
  exact surjective_etaleFundamentalGroupMap_of_finite_isClosed_compl _
    (finite_compl_range_chainOpen_homOfLE x hclosed j)
    (isClosed_singleton_of_notMem_range_chainOpen_homOfLE x hclosed j) hconnected w

end Surjective

end AlgebraicGeometry

